            {
              "id": "1l6D-6ktrjkZigb6w-OndM75yatC3s-u3",
              "name": "Endocrine-Thyroid disorder.pdf",
              "folder": "Chapter 12 Endocrine",
              "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l6D-6ktrjkZigb6w-OndM75yatC3s-u3/view",
              "totalPages": 54,
              "rawText": "FILE: Endocrine-Thyroid disorder.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 12 Endocrine\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l6D-6ktrjkZigb6w-OndM75yatC3s-u3/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 54\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nTHYROID \nDISORDERS\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D., DIP CLI EPI.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nThyroid hormone regulation\n2\nThyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)\nThyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH)\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nThyroid synthesis\n3\nRef: http://www.pathwaymedicine.org/thyroid-hormone-synthesis\nNa+/I- symporter\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nThyroid synthesis\n5\nBerne et al, Physiology 5th Edition.\nIodination\nOrganification\nCoupling\nThyroid peroxidase (TPO)\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nThyroid synthesis\n6\nActive hormone Inactive hormone\nAlanine side chain\nLiver, Kidney, Gut and \nPeripheral tissue\nHarrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 19th Edition\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nThyroid hormone metabolism\n7\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nEffect of thyroid hormone\nTarget tissue Effect Mechanism\nHeart\nChronotropic Increase number and affinity of beta-adrenergic receptor.\nInotropic Enhance responses to circulating catecholamine.\nAdipose tissue Catabolic Stimulate lipolysis.\nMuscle Catabolic Increase protein breakdown.\nBone Development Promote normal growth and skeleton development.\nNervous system Development Promote normal brain development.\nGut Metabolic Increase rate of carbohydrate absorption.\nOther Calorigenic Increase metabolic rate and oxygen consumption.\n9\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nThyroid binding protein\n10\nThyroxine binding \nglobulin (TBG)\n▪ Pregnancy\n▪ Contraceptive\n▪ Chronic active hepatitis\n▪ Drug: methadone, heroin, \nperphenazine\n▪ Malnutrition\n▪ Cirrhosis\n▪ Drug: androgen, \nglucocorticoids, salicylate, \nphenylbutazone\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nThyroid function tests\nNormal Hypothyroid Hyperthyroid\nTotal T4 4.5-10.9 mcg/dL ↓ ↑\nTotal T3 60-181 ng/dL -/↓ ↑\nFree T4 0.8-2.7 ng/dL ↓ ↑\nFree T3 230-619 pg/dL ↓ ↑\nTSH 0.5-4.7 mIU/L ↑/↓ ↑/↓\n123I uptake \nRadio active iodine \nuptake (RAIU)\n5-35% ↓ ↑\n11\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nThyroid disorders\n12\nNormalHyperthyroidism Hypothyroidism\n▪ Antithyroid drugs\n▪ Radiation\n▪ Surgery\n▪ Thyroid hormone\nEuthyroid\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nHypothyroidism\n▪ Primary hypothyroidism\n▪ Hashimoto’s thyroiditis*\n▪ Iodine deficiency*\n▪ Drug induced\n▪ Radiation\n▪ Thyroid surgery\n▪ Secondary hypothyroidism\n13\nT4 FT4 T3 FT3 TSH Diagnosis\n↓ ↓ ↓ ↓ ↑ Hypothyroidism\n↑ Subclinical \nhypothyroidism\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\nHypothyroidism\n▪ Hashimoto’s thyroiditis\n▪ Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies (TgAb)\n▪ Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies (TPOAb)\n▪ TSH receptor antibodies (TSHRAb)\n14\nATA/AACE Guidelines for Hypothyroidism in Adults, Endocr Pract. 2012;18(No. 6) e9\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nHypothyroidism\n▪ Cretinism\n“Congenital hypothyroidism is inadequate \nthyroid hormone production in newborn \ninfants.”\n➢Iodine deficiency\n➢Drug induced: para-amino salicylic acid \n(PAS), phenylbutazone\n15\nDaniel MS, Congenital Hypothyroidism. 2015. available at http://emedicine.medscape.com\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nSign & Symptoms\n16\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nTreatment\n▪ General agreement that patients with primary \nhypothyroidism with TSH levels above 10 mIU/L should \nbe treated.\n▪ Levothyroxine recommended for treating \nhypothyroidism\n18\nATA/AACE Guidelines for Hypothyroidism in Adults, Endocr Pract. 2012;18(No. 6) e9\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nLevothyroxine (L-thyroxine)\n• Drug of choice for thyroid hormone replacement and \nsuppressive therapy \n• It is chemically stable, relatively inexpensive, free of \nantigenicity, and has uniform potency. \n• Take before meal 30 minutes \n19\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nLevothyroxine (L-thyroxine)\n• Causes of impaired levothyroxine absorption\n20\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nLevothyroxine (L-thyroxine)\n• Adverse drug reaction\n• Tachycardia\n• Heart failure\n• Angina pectoris\n• Myocardial infarction\n• Decrease bone mineral density and increase risk of bone \nfracture\n21\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\nLiothyronine\n• Synthetic T3 \n• Short half-life 1.5 days\n• Higher incidence of cardiac adverse effects, higher \ncost.\n22\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\nLiotrix\n• Synthetic T4:T3 in a 4:1 ratio\n• Is chemically stable, pure, and has a predictable \npotency but is expensive.\n23\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nMyxedema coma\n• เป็นภาวะขาดไทรอยด์รุนแรง พบได้น้อยแต่รุนแรงถึงแก่ชีวิต\n• อาการได้แก่ สับสน ซึม ไม่รู้สึกตัว ตัวเย็น (hypothermia) เพ้อ โคม่า \n• การรักษา levothyroxine IV 300-500 mcg แล้วปรับลดลงเหลือ 75-100 mcg ร่วมกับ \nhydrocortisone IV 100 mcg ทุก 8 ชั่วโมง\n• ระดับ TSH ควรลดลงอยู่ในระดับปกติภายใน 24 ชั่วโมง\n• เมื่อผู้ป่ วยมีอาการดีขึ้นจึงเปลี่ยนเป็นยารับประทาน\n24\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nThyrotoxicosis\n▪ Hyperthyroidism\n▪ Graves' disease* (primary)\n▪ TSH- producing pituitary  tumor (secondary)\n▪ Toxic multinodular goiter\n▪ Toxic adenoma\n▪ Jod-Basedow (Iodine induced) \n▪ Thyroiditis\n25\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nThyrotoxicosis\n26\nT4 FT4 T3 FT3 TSH Diagnosis\n↑ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↓ Hyperthyroidism\n↑ ↑ ↓ T3 toxicosis\n↑ ↑\n ↓ T4 toxicosis\n↓ Subclinical\nhyperthyroidism\n↑ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑ Secondary\nhyperthyroidism\n(pituitary tumor)\n\n--- [Page 24] ---\nThyrotoxicosis\n▪ Graves' disease\n▪ Results from the action of thyroid -stimulating antibodies \n(TSAb) or thyroid stimulating immunoglobulin (TSI) directed \nagainst the thyrotropin receptor on the surface of thyroid \ncell. \n▪ These immunoglobulins bind to the receptor and activate \nthe enzyme adenylate cyclase in the same manner as TSH.\n27\n\n--- [Page 25] ---\nGraves' disease\n28\nTSI or TSAb\n\n--- [Page 26] ---\nSign & Symptoms\n29\n\n--- [Page 27] ---\nSign & Symptoms\n• Diffuse goiter\n• Lid retraction \n• Lid lag \n• Thyroid acropachy\n• Onycholysis\n• Exophthalmos (Grave disease)\n• Localized  myxedema (Grave disease)\n30\n\n--- [Page 28] ---\nSign & Symptoms\n31\n\n--- [Page 29] ---\nTreatment options\n▪ Antithyroid drugs (ATDs)\n▪ Radioactive iodine (131I)\n▪ Thyroidectomy\n32\nSubclinical hyperthyroidism\n▪ อายุ > 65 ปี มี TSH <0.1 mIU/L เป็นเวลานาน\n▪ อายุ < 65 ปี ที่มีความเสี่ยงต่อการเกิดโรคหัวใจและหลอดเลือด\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\nHyperthyroidism Hypothyroidism\n\n--- [Page 30] ---\nThioamide or Thioureas\n• Propylthiouracil (PTU) \n6-propyl-2-thiouracil\n• Methimazole (MMI) \n1-methyl-2-mercaptoimidazole\n33\n6 2\nไม่มีหมู่แทนที่ ท ำให้ยับยั้ง\nกำรเปลี่ยน T4 → T3 ได้\n\n--- [Page 31] ---\nThioamide or Thioureas\n• Mechanism of action\n• Inhibiting the peroxidase ‐catalyzed reactions necessary for \niodine organification.\n• PTU inhibit peripheral deiodination (inhibit T4 →T3)\n34\nPTU MMI\nWater solubility Low High\nHalf-life 1-2.5 hours 6-9 hours\nRelative Potency 1 10\nProtein bound 60-80% No\nPlacenta Low High\nBreast Low High\nUrinary excretion 35% 10%\n\n--- [Page 32] ---\nThioamide or Thioureas\n• Adverse drug reactions\n• Common: urticarial, skin rash, joint pain, nausea, vomiting, \n• Serious:\n• Agranulocytosis: ANC < 500 cells/mm3\n• PTU: dose independent\n• MMI: dose dependent (> 30 mg/day)\n• Hepatotoxicity\n• PTU: hepatocellular type\n• MMI: cholestasis type\n• Lupus like syndrome (PTU)\n• Vasculitis (PTU)\n35\n\n--- [Page 33] ---\nThioamide or Thioureas\n• Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)\nANC = (%Neutrophil + Band) X WBC\n100\n36\nEx. WBC = 7.0 x103 cell/mm3\nNeutrophil = 50%\nANC = (50% + 0) x 7.0 x103 cell/mm3 \n100\n\n--- [Page 34] ---\nBeta blockers\n• Recommended in all patients with symptom of \nhyperthyroidism, especially \n• Elderly patients\n• Thyrotoxic patients with resting heart rates in excess of 90 \nbeats per minute \n• Coexistent cardiovascular disease.\n37\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\n“Adjunctive therapy with thioamides or iodides \nto treat hyperthyroidism”\n\n--- [Page 35] ---\nBeta blockers\n• Mechanism of action\n• Inhibit sympathetic activity\n• Some beta adrenergic blocking agent may block T4 → T3 \nconversion\n• Propranolol\n• Nadolol\n38\n\n--- [Page 36] ---\nBeta blockers\n39\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\n\n--- [Page 37] ---\nIodides\n• Lugol’s solution or strong iodine solution \n• Saturated Solution of Potassium Iodide (SSKI) \n• Mechanism of action\n• Inhibit organification and hormone release.\n• Decreases the size and vascularity of the hyperplastic gland,\n40\nUseful in the preparation of patients for thyroidectomy or thyroid \nstorm\n\n--- [Page 38] ---\nIodides\n• Adverse drug reactions\n• Skin rash\n• Fever \n• Conjunctivitis\n• Salivary gland swelling and inflammation\n41\nยำมีผลในระยะสั้นประมำณ 2-4 สัปดำห์ หลังจำกนั้นกำรรักษำจะไม่ได้ผล เนื่องจำกเกิด escaped phenomenon\n\n--- [Page 39] ---\nDexamethasone\n• Mechanism of action\n• Inhibit hormone release.\n• Inhibit peripheral deiodination\n• Useful in thyroid storm\n• Not recommended long duration\n42\n\n--- [Page 40] ---\nLithium carbonate\n• Mechanism of action\n• Inhibit hormone release.\n• Alternative choice\n43\nส่วนใหญ่มักจะใช้เป็ นกำรชั่วครำวก่อนที่จะท ำกำรรักษำด้วยวิธีอื่น เช่น ผ่ำตัด หรือ radioactive iodine\n\n--- [Page 41] ---\nClinical Situations\n44\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\nGrave ophthalmopathy (GO)\n\n--- [Page 42] ---\nRadioactive iodine\n• High efficacy (80-90%)\n• RAI therapy: Women planning a pregnancy in the \nfuture (>6 months)\n• RAI therapy: Definite contraindications include \n• Pregnancy\n• Congenital hypothyroidism\n• Congenital malformation\n• Lactation\n• Coexisting thyroid cancer\n• Young patient\n45\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\n\n--- [Page 43] ---\nThyroidectomy\n• Recommended\n• Women planning a pregnancy in <6 months \n• Large goiters (≥ 80 g)\n• Patients with moderate to severe active grave \nophthalmopathy (GO).\n• Complications of thyroidectomy\n• Hypoparathyroidism\n• Permanent hypothyroidism → thyroid replacement\n• Vocal cord paralysis\n46\nAvoid in Patients with right pulmonary hypertension, or congestive heart failure\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\n\n--- [Page 44] ---\nThyroid storm or crisis\n• เป็นภาวะต่อมไทรอยด์ท างานเกินขั้นรุนแรง\n• ผู้ป ่ วยมักมีไข้ อัตราการเต้นของหัวใจเร็ว อัตราการหายใจเร็ว ภาวะขาดน ้า เพ้อ \nคลื่นไส้ อาเจียน อุจจาระร่วงและโคม่า\n• ปัจจัยกระตุ้นได้แก่\n• การผ่าตัด\n• การบาดเจ็บ การติดเชื้อ\n• ขาดยาต้านไทรอยด์\n• ได้รับการรักษาด้วยสารกัมมันตรังสี\n47\n\n--- [Page 45] ---\nThyroid storm or crisis\n48\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\n\n--- [Page 46] ---\nThyroid storm or crisis\n49\nATA Guidelines. Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis 2016.\nพิจำรณำยำหลำยชนิดร่วมกันเพื่อช่วยลดกำรเกิด hyperthyroidism\n\n--- [Page 47] ---\nAmiodarone\n50\n37% iodine by mass\nAmiodarone Induced Thyrotoxicosis (AIT) \nAmiodarone Induced Hypothyroidism (AIH)\n\n--- [Page 48] ---\nThyroid scan\n51\n\n--- [Page 49] ---\nAmiodarone Induced Hypothyroidism\n52\n2018 European Thyroid Association (ETA) Guidelines for the Management of Amiodarone-Associated Thyroid Dysfunction\nThyroid scan\n\n--- [Page 50] ---\nAmiodarone Induced Thyrotoxicosis\n• AIT-1 (Iodine induced or Jod-basedow phenomenon)\n• Common in low iodine intake country\n• AIT-2 (Destructive thyroiditis)\n53\n2018 European Thyroid Association (ETA) Guidelines for the Management of Amiodarone-Associated Thyroid Dysfunction\n\n--- [Page 51] ---\nAmiodarone Induced Thyrotoxicosis\n54\n2018 European Thyroid Association (ETA) Guidelines for the Management of Amiodarone-Associated Thyroid Dysfunction\n\n--- [Page 52] ---\nGoiter\n55\n\n--- [Page 53] ---\nGoitrogen\n56"
            }
